# Converting Spring Security WebMVC Config to WebFlux

The migration is **not a 1:1 rename**. Reactive Spring Security uses a parallel set of classes — most are prefixed with `Server...` and return `Mono<Void>`. The foundational swaps are:

```
HttpSecurity              → ServerHttpSecurity
SecurityFilterChain       → SecurityWebFilterChain
@EnableWebSecurity        → @EnableWebFluxSecurity
AuthenticationSuccessHandler → ServerAuthenticationSuccessHandler
AuthenticationFailureHandler → ServerAuthenticationFailureHandler
LogoutHandler             → ServerLogoutHandler
HttpSession               → WebSession
```

Below is each configurer you listed, mapped to its reactive form.

---

## 1. `OAuth2LoginConfigurer<HttpSecurity>` → `oauth2Login` on `ServerHttpSecurity`

The reactive `OAuth2LoginSpec` is flatter than the servlet version. There is **no `tokenEndpoint()` block** and **no `defaultSuccessUrl()`** — those collapse into a single success-handler slot and a bean, respectively.

| WebMVC method | WebFlux equivalent |
|---|---|
| `redirectionEndpoint().baseUri(...)` | `.authenticationMatcher(ServerWebExchangeMatcher)` |
| `failureHandler(...)` | `.authenticationFailureHandler(ServerAuthenticationFailureHandler)` |
| `defaultSuccessUrl("/home")` | `new RedirectServerAuthenticationSuccessHandler("/home")` |
| `successHandler(...)` | `.authenticationSuccessHandler(ServerAuthenticationSuccessHandler)` |
| `tokenEndpoint().accessTokenResponseClient(...)` | Register a `ReactiveOAuth2AccessTokenResponseClient` **bean** |

```java
RedirectServerAuthenticationSuccessHandler successHandler =
    new RedirectServerAuthenticationSuccessHandler("/home");

// Optional: mimic defaultSuccessUrl("/home", true) — always redirect, ignore saved request
successHandler.setRequestCache(NoOpServerRequestCache.getInstance());

http.oauth2Login(oauth2 -> oauth2
    // redirectionEndpoint().baseUri("/login/oauth2/code/*")
    .authenticationMatcher(new PathPatternParserServerWebExchangeMatcher(
            "/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}"))
    // failureHandler(...)
    .authenticationFailureHandler(
            new RedirectServerAuthenticationFailureHandler("/login?error"))
    // defaultSuccessUrl(...) / successHandler(...)  — these merge into ONE slot
    .authenticationSuccessHandler(successHandler)
);
```

**Important:** `defaultSuccessUrl` and `successHandler` both map to `authenticationSuccessHandler` — you can only set one, so combine the behavior you need into a single handler.

For the **token endpoint**, expose a bean instead of configuring it inline:

```java
@Bean
ReactiveOAuth2AccessTokenResponseClient<OAuth2AuthorizationCodeGrantRequest>
authorizationCodeAccessTokenResponseClient() {
    WebClientReactiveAuthorizationCodeTokenResponseClient client =
        new WebClientReactiveAuthorizationCodeTokenResponseClient();
    // client.addParametersConverter(...);  // customize request
    // client.setWebClient(...);            // customize WebClient
    return client;
}
```

The default reactive authorization-response endpoint is `/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}`; if you change the matcher, your `ClientRegistration.redirectUri` must match it too.

