## 2. `LogoutConfigurer<HttpSecurity>` → `logout` on `ServerHttpSecurity`

The reactive `LogoutSpec` is small: it has `logoutUrl`, `requiresLogout`, `logoutHandler`, and `logoutSuccessHandler` — but **no** `invalidateHttpSession`, `clearAuthentication`, or `deleteCookies`. You reconstruct those by composing `ServerLogoutHandler`s with `DelegatingServerLogoutHandler`.

| WebMVC method | WebFlux equivalent |
|---|---|
| `logoutUrl("/logout")` | `.logoutUrl("/logout")` |
| `clearAuthentication(true)` | `SecurityContextServerLogoutHandler` (this is the default) |
| `invalidateHttpSession(true)` | `WebSessionServerLogoutHandler` |
| `deleteCookies(...)` | Custom `ServerLogoutHandler` that adds expired cookies |
| `addLogoutHandler(h)` | `.logoutHandler(h)` — but it **replaces**, so wrap multiples in `DelegatingServerLogoutHandler` |

```java
ServerLogoutHandler logoutHandler = new DelegatingServerLogoutHandler(
    new SecurityContextServerLogoutHandler(),   // clearAuthentication(true)
    new WebSessionServerLogoutHandler(),         // invalidateHttpSession(true)
    clearCookies("SESSION", "XSRF-TOKEN"),       // deleteCookies(...)
    customServerLogoutHandler()                  // addLogoutHandler(...)
);

http.logout(logout -> logout
    .logoutUrl("/logout")
    .logoutHandler(logoutHandler)
);
```

```java
private ServerLogoutHandler clearCookies(String... cookieNames) {
    return (exchange, authentication) -> {
        ServerHttpResponse response = exchange.getExchange().getResponse();
        for (String name : cookieNames) {
            response.addCookie(ResponseCookie.from(name, "")
                .path("/")
                .maxAge(Duration.ZERO)
                .build());
        }
        return Mono.empty();
    };
}
```

> Note: WebFlux uses `WebSession` (default cookie name **`SESSION`**), not the servlet `JSESSIONID` — adjust your cookie name accordingly.

