## 3. `SessionManagementConfigurer<HttpSecurity>` → mostly redesign

This is the **biggest gap**. WebFlux has no full session-management configurer; session-context behavior is governed by the `ServerSecurityContextRepository`.

| WebMVC method | WebFlux equivalent |
|---|---|
| `sessionCreationPolicy(STATELESS)` | `http.securityContextRepository(NoOpServerSecurityContextRepository.getInstance())` |
| `sessionCreationPolicy(IF_REQUIRED)` (default) | default `WebSessionServerSecurityContextRepository` |
| `sessionCreationPolicy(ALWAYS)` | no direct equivalent |
| `invalidateSessionStrategy(...)` | **no equivalent** — invalidate manually |
| `sessionFixation(...)` | **no setting** — handled implicitly (session id changes when the context is saved) |
| `sessionConcurrency(...)` | `sessionManagement(...).concurrentSessions(...)` (recent versions only) |

**Creation policy / stateless:**

```java
// sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS)
http.securityContextRepository(NoOpServerSecurityContextRepository.getInstance());
```

> Caveat: `oauth2Login()` is inherently **stateful** (the authorization request/state and the authenticated user are stored in the `WebSession`). If you actually need a stateless API, use `oauth2ResourceServer().jwt()` rather than `oauth2Login()` with a NoOp repository.

**Session fixation:** there is no toggle — the reactive `WebSessionServerSecurityContextRepository` rotates the session id when it persists the `SecurityContext`, which is the reactive analogue of fixation protection.

**Invalidate session:** no `invalidateSessionStrategy`. Use `WebSessionServerLogoutHandler` during logout, or invalidate explicitly:

```java
exchange.getSession().flatMap(WebSession::invalidate);
```

**Concurrency** (requires a recent Spring Security version — reactive concurrent-session control is relatively new, ~6.3+):

```java
@Bean
ReactiveSessionRegistry reactiveSessionRegistry() {
    return new InMemoryReactiveSessionRegistry();
}

http.sessionManagement(session -> session
    .concurrentSessions(concurrency -> concurrency
        .maximumSessions(SessionLimit.of(1))
        .sessionRegistry(reactiveSessionRegistry)
    )
);
```

> One subtlety: if you **replace** the `oauth2Login` `authenticationSuccessHandler` (as in section 1), you may bypass the success handler that concurrent-session control wires in automatically. If your version supports the `Consumer<List<ServerAuthenticationSuccessHandler>>` overload, customize the existing handler list instead of replacing it outright.

