## 4. `OAuth2ClientConfigurer.authorizationCodeGrant()` → `oauth2Client` on `ServerHttpSecurity`

The reactive `OAuth2ClientSpec` is **flat** — there is **no nested `authorizationCodeGrant()` builder**. The methods sit directly on the spec.

| WebMVC | WebFlux |
|---|---|
| `oauth2Client().authorizationCodeGrant().authorizationRequestResolver(...)` | `oauth2Client(o -> o.authorizationRequestResolver(...))` |
| `...authorizationCodeGrant().authorizationRequestRepository(...)` | `...authorizationRequestRepository(...)` |
| `...authorizationCodeGrant().accessTokenResponseClient(...)` | `ReactiveOAuth2AccessTokenResponseClient` **bean** (same as §1) |

```java
http.oauth2Client(oauth2 -> oauth2
    .authorizationRequestResolver(serverAuthorizationRequestResolver())
    .authorizationRequestRepository(serverAuthorizationRequestRepository())
    // .authenticationManager(...)   // if you need a custom code-exchange manager
);
```

The default reactive authorization-request path is `/oauth2/authorization/{registrationId}`, handled by `DefaultServerOAuth2AuthorizationRequestResolver`. The actual token exchange and authorized-client management live in `ReactiveOAuth2AuthorizedClientManager` / `ReactiveOAuth2AuthorizedClientProvider` beans rather than inline config.

