## Complete combined example

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebFluxSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    SecurityWebFilterChain securityWebFilterChain(
            ServerHttpSecurity http,
            ReactiveSessionRegistry reactiveSessionRegistry) {

        RedirectServerAuthenticationSuccessHandler successHandler =
            new RedirectServerAuthenticationSuccessHandler("/home");
        successHandler.setRequestCache(NoOpServerRequestCache.getInstance());

        ServerLogoutHandler logoutHandler = new DelegatingServerLogoutHandler(
            new SecurityContextServerLogoutHandler(),
            new WebSessionServerLogoutHandler(),
            clearCookies("SESSION", "XSRF-TOKEN"),
            customServerLogoutHandler()
        );

        http
            .authorizeExchange(ex -> ex.anyExchange().authenticated())

            .oauth2Login(oauth2 -> oauth2
                .authenticationMatcher(new PathPatternParserServerWebExchangeMatcher(
                        "/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}"))
                .authenticationFailureHandler(
                        new RedirectServerAuthenticationFailureHandler("/login?error"))
                .authenticationSuccessHandler(successHandler))

            .oauth2Client(oauth2 -> oauth2
                .authorizationRequestResolver(serverAuthorizationRequestResolver())
                .authorizationRequestRepository(serverAuthorizationRequestRepository()))

            .logout(logout -> logout
                .logoutUrl("/logout")
                .logoutHandler(logoutHandler))

            // STATELESS would instead be:
            // .securityContextRepository(NoOpServerSecurityContextRepository.getInstance())
            .sessionManagement(session -> session
                .concurrentSessions(c -> c
                    .maximumSessions(SessionLimit.of(1))
                    .sessionRegistry(reactiveSessionRegistry)));

        return http.build();
    }

    @Bean
    ReactiveSessionRegistry reactiveSessionRegistry() {
        return new InMemoryReactiveSessionRegistry();
    }

    @Bean
    ReactiveOAuth2AccessTokenResponseClient<OAuth2AuthorizationCodeGrantRequest>
    authorizationCodeAccessTokenResponseClient() {
        return new WebClientReactiveAuthorizationCodeTokenResponseClient();
    }

    private ServerLogoutHandler clearCookies(String... names) {
        return (exchange, auth) -> {
            ServerHttpResponse resp = exchange.getExchange().getResponse();
            for (String n : names) {
                resp.addCookie(ResponseCookie.from(n, "").path("/")
                        .maxAge(Duration.ZERO).build());
            }
            return Mono.empty();
        };
    }

    private ServerLogoutHandler customServerLogoutHandler() {
        return (exchange, auth) -> Mono.empty(); // your logic
    }

    private ServerOAuth2AuthorizationRequestResolver serverAuthorizationRequestResolver() {
        return null; // your resolver
    }

    private ServerAuthorizationRequestRepository<OAuth2AuthorizationRequest>
    serverAuthorizationRequestRepository() {
        return null; // e.g. new WebSessionServerOAuth2AuthorizationRequestRepository()
    }
}
```

---

## Key takeaways

1. **Not a 1:1 mapping.** `successHandler`/`defaultSuccessUrl` merge into one `authenticationSuccessHandler` slot; the logout sub-flags become composed `ServerLogoutHandler`s; the OAuth2 token endpoint becomes a `ReactiveOAuth2AccessTokenResponseClient` bean.
2. **Session management is the weakest area.** `invalidateSessionStrategy` and `sessionFixation` have no direct reactive equivalents, `sessionCreationPolicy` becomes a `ServerSecurityContextRepository` choice, and `sessionConcurrency` requires a recent Spring Security version.
3. **`authorizationCodeGrant()` disappears** — its methods live directly on the flat `oauth2Client(...)` spec.
4. **Dependencies:** add `spring-boot-starter-webflux` + `spring-security-oauth2-client` and remove the servlet (`spring-boot-starter-web`) starter — don't keep both stacks on the classpath.

If you share your exact **Spring Security / Spring Boot version**, I can confirm which version-gated features (especially reactive concurrent-session control) are actually available to you.

